.TE
.PP
Persist the combined startup sequence across future sessions by setting
\f[CR]init_moods\f[R] inside an active Koshka session.
.IP
.EX
koshconf set init_moods kosh,bash \-\-persist
.EE
.PP
Inspect \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] before persisting \f[CR]init_moods\f[R] if
the configuration was generated with \f[CR]koshconf create kosh\f[R].
That preset already sources Bash startup files directly inside
\f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R].
Enable only one loading mechanism so the Bash startup files are not
executed twice.
.SH EXECUTE BASH SCRIPTS THAT FAIL STRICT VALIDATION
.nf
\&
.fi
Run a Bash script under the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood when the default
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood rejects its syntax.
.IP
.EX
kosh \-\-mood bash script.bash
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-mood bash\f[R] flag selects the Bash compatibility mood
for that execution.
\f[CR]bash script.bash\f[R] uses the first \f[CR]bash\f[R] in
\f[CR]PATH\f[R]; use the original Bash executable\(aqs absolute path to
bypass a Koshka link.
A script with a \f[CR]#!/bin/bash\f[R] shebang still runs
\f[CR]/bin/bash\f[R], unless mimicry (\f[CR]\-I\f[R]) runs the script
inside Koshka.
.SH RUN KOSHKA UNDER THE NAME BASH
.nf
\&
.fi
A link named \f[CR]bash\f[R] to the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] binary starts Koshka
in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood with the Bash startup files.
Place it in a directory that precedes the installed Bash in
\f[CR]PATH\f[R].
\f[CR]assimilate \-\-link\-mood=bash\f[R] creates the same link.
.IP
.EX
ln \-s \(dq$(command \-v kosh)\(dq \(ti/.local/bin/bash
.EE
.PP
A configured \f[CR]mood\f[R] does not override the link name, but
\f[CR]init_moods=kosh,bash\f[R] also sources \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R].
\f[CR]bash \-\-version\f[R] prints the Koshka banner.
A shebang with an absolute path, such as \f[CR]#!/bin/bash\f[R], still
runs the installed Bash.
.SH USE KOSHKA AS A BASH\-COMPATIBLE LOGIN SHELL
.nf
\&
.fi
On Unix, install Koshka under \f[CR]/usr/local/bin/kosh\f[R] and select
\f[CR]/usr/local/bin/bash\f[R] as your login shell to start Koshka in
the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood.
Leave the system Bash binary intact.
Keep your current Bash session open, and record the absolute path of
your current login shell
(\f[CR]printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(dq$SHELL\(dq\f[R] in the original login
session) for rollback.
.PP
\f[B]Safety prerequisite:\f[R] Before changing the login shell, verify
that you can log in as \f[CR]root\f[R] with its password and restore
your account\(aqs previous shell.
If direct root login is disabled, as on a default macOS installation,
verify that you can log in to a separate administrator account and use
\f[CR]sudo\f[R] there.
Do not proceed if your only recovery path depends on the account whose
shell you are changing.
.IP "1." 3
Copy the verified executable into \f[CR]/usr/local/bin\f[R], or link to
a stable executable in a directory managed by the system administrator.
Create \f[CR]/usr/local/bin\f[R] first if it does not exist.
If \f[CR]/usr/local/bin/kosh\f[R] is already a trusted Koshka
installation, reuse it.
Do not overwrite existing files or links at either destination.
.IP
.EX
sudo mkdir \-p /usr/local/bin
sudo install \-m 0755 /absolute/path/to/kosh /usr/local/bin/kosh
.EE
.PP
To link instead of copying, use this command in place of
\f[CR]install\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
sudo ln \-s /absolute/path/to/kosh /usr/local/bin/kosh
.EE
.IP "2." 3
Create the \f[CR]bash\f[R] link and check its version and interactive
login startup before changing your account.
.IP
.EX
sudo ln \-s /usr/local/bin/kosh /usr/local/bin/bash
/usr/local/bin/bash \-\-version
/usr/local/bin/bash \-l \-i
.EE
.PP
At the trial shell prompt, run the following commands.
The version must end in \f[CR]\-kosh\f[R].
Exit the trial shell to continue.
.IP
.EX
printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(dq$BASH_VERSION\(dq
exit
.EE
.IP "3." 3
Add the login shell path to \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R] if it is not already
listed, then change your own login shell.
Open a new login session to verify that it starts Koshka before closing
your original session.
.IP
.EX
\f[B](\f[R]grep \-Fxq /usr/local/bin/bash /etc/shells \f[B]||\f[R]
  printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq /usr/local/bin/bash \f[B]|\f[R] sudo tee \-a /etc/shells\f[B])\f[R] \f[B]&&\f[R]
  chsh \-s /usr/local/bin/bash
.EE
.PP
This changes the login shell for your account.
If \f[CR]/usr/local/bin\f[R] also precedes the directory containing the
original Bash in \f[CR]PATH\f[R], it changes what \f[CR]bash\f[R] and
\f[CR]#!/usr/bin/env bash\f[R] resolve to.
It does not change an absolute shebang such as \f[CR]#!/bin/bash\f[R].
.SH SET KOSHKA AS THE DEFAULT LOGIN SHELL
.nf
\&
.fi
.IP "1." 3
Verify that the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] binary is reachable in \f[CR]PATH\f[R]
and executes cleanly from the current Bash session.
.IP
.EX
command \-v kosh
kosh \-\-version
.EE
.IP "2." 3
Register the absolute path of \f[CR]kosh\f[R] in \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R]
and update the user login shell with \f[CR]chsh\f[R] on Unix systems.
.IP
.EX
kosh_path=\(dq$(command \-v kosh)\(dq
grep \-Fx \(dq$kosh_path\(dq /etc/shells \f[B]||\f[R] printf \(aq%s\(rsn\(aq \(dq$kosh_path\(dq \f[B]|\f[R] sudo tee \-a /etc/shells
chsh \-s \(dq$(command \-v kosh)\(dq
.EE
.IP "3." 3
Sign out of the system session and sign back in to activate the new
login shell.
If a terminal emulator overrides the system login shell with a custom
command, update the profile settings inside that terminal application.
On Windows, \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R] and \f[CR]chsh\f[R] are not used.
Configure Koshka directly as a terminal profile on Windows.
.SH REVERT THE LOGIN SHELL TO BASH
.nf
\&
.fi
.IP "1." 3
Keep the original Bash executable installed.
From an active session, confirm that the original shell\(aqs absolute
path is present in \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R], then use that path with
\f[CR]chsh\f[R].
Do not use \f[CR]command \-v bash\f[R] for rollback: it may return the
Koshka link.
The following example assumes that the original shell was
\f[CR]/bin/bash\f[R].
.IP
.EX
chsh \-s /bin/bash
.EE
.PP
If the new shell prevents you from logging in, log in as \f[CR]root\f[R]
and restore the recorded shell for the affected account.
From a separate administrator account, run the same command with
\f[CR]sudo\f[R].
Replace \f[CR]your_username\f[R] and \f[CR]/bin/bash\f[R] with the
affected account name and the previous shell\(aqs absolute path.
.IP
.EX
chsh \-s /bin/bash your_username
.EE
.IP "2." 3
Open a new login session and verify that it starts the original Bash.
.IP "3." 3
If no account or terminal profile uses the Koshka link, remove
\f[CR]/usr/local/bin/bash\f[R] and its \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R] entry if
you added them.
Remove any direct Koshka startup commands from terminal profiles before
deleting the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] binary.
.SH UNINSTALL THE KOSHKA BINARY
.nf
\&
.fi
.IP "1." 3
Revert the system login shell and terminal emulator profiles to another
shell before removing Koshka.
.IP "2." 3
Remove the binary using the installation method that matches the host
environment.
.PP
